(i) The product of $\phi_1$, $\phi_2$, and $\phi_3$ equals $0$;
(ii) The sum of $\phi_1$, $\phi_2$, and $\phi_3$ is greater than $1$;
(iii A has a cardinality exceeding $|G:/\phi_1\times\phi_2\times\phi_3|+|A|$.
Furthermore, it is shown that this result is almost sharp by proving the existence of a set $A$ for any given group $G$, such that the sumset $A + \phi_1(A) + \phi_2(A) + \phi_3(A)$ contains a Bohr set under the conditions specified above, and the size of $A$ is only marginally larger than the size of $G$. The final result is based on a broader conclusion that the exponent $\gamma(G,\phi)$ of a given group $G$ and endomorphism $\phi$ will experience a decrease as the size of $G$ approaches infinity, implying the presence of Bohr sets in sumsets within $G$ for many values of $N$. More specifically, the value of $\gamma(G,\phi)$ will tend towards $0$ as the size of $G$ increases without bound. The standard exponent $\gamma(G)$ of the group $G$ is then defined as the minimum of the exponents $\gamma(G,\phi)$ over all endomorphisms $\phi$ in $G$. The value of $\gamma(G)$ is known to be $0$ for $G = \mathbb{Z}^n$ when $n > 1$. The size of this minimum is $1/3$ and it is shown that this value is almost sharp in various cases involving odd numbers. The bounds for $\gamma(G)$ have also been increased, improved and expanded upon within this work.

